<title>USA: Senators complain Philippines blocking pork import.</title>
<headline>Senators complain Philippines blocking pork import.</headline>
<dateline>WASHINGTON 1997-07-10</dateline>
<text>
<p>Two midwestern senators said Thursday they would seek disciplinary action by the World Trade Organization unless the Philippine government lives up to its promise to import pork and poultry from U.S. producers.</p>
<p>Tim Johnson of South Dakota and Bob Kerry of Nebraska, both Democrats, said they sent a letter to the Philippine ambassador demanding a meeting to discuss U.S. exports.</p>
<p>&quot;Their blatant disregard of trading rules is unacceptable. They have an obligation to meet and there can be no more stalling,&quot; Johnson said in a statement.</p>
<p>Some 58 lawmakers complained last year that the Philippine government had blocked U.S. imports of pork and poultry.</p>
<p>Johnson said if the Philippines failed to meet its trade obligations, he would ask U.S. Trade Representative Charlene Barshefsky to take action, possibly by asking the WTO to impose disciplinary measures against the Philippines.</p>
<p>((Washington commodities desk 202 898 8467))</p>
